---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { categories } from '../data/categories';
import { getAllArms } from '../lib/arms';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const arms = await getAllArms();
---

<Base>
  <header class="headline">
    <p class="kicker">Of Interest to the Living Historian</p>
    <h1>The Arms That Fought the War</h1>
    <hr class="rule-short" />
    <p class="deck">Muskets, Rifles, Carbines &amp; Revolvers of the Union and the Confederacy</p>
    <hr class="rule-short" />
    <p class="deck deck--minor">
      A reference to their making, their use in the field, and their proper carrying by the reenactor
      of the present day.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="lead">
    <div class="prose dropcap">
      <p>
        When the war opened in the spring of 1861, neither government could arm the men who came
        forward. The Federal arsenals held a few hundred thousand serviceable muskets, many of them
        old smoothbores converted from flintlock, and a good share of those stood in Southern arsenals
        that were promptly seized. Within a year both sides had sent purchasing agents across the
        Atlantic, set private contractors to work, and pressed into service nearly anything that would
        take a cap and throw a ball.
      </p>
      <p>
        By 1863 the picture had settled. The infantryman of either army most likely carried a
        .58-caliber Springfield or a .577 Enfield rifle-musket, loaded from the muzzle with a paper
        cartridge and a hollow-based Minié ball. Around him, though, the variety persisted: Austrian
        Lorenz rifles in the Western armies, Belgian and Prussian muskets in the regiments of
        1861 and 1862, and, in growing numbers among the Federal cavalry, breech-loading and
        repeating carbines that would change the business of soldiering for good.
      </p>
      <p>
        These pages take up each of those arms in turn. Every article gives the particulars of the
        piece, an account of its making and service, and a section addressed to the reenactor: which
        reproductions are made, which impressions the arm suits, and what to mind in carrying it on
        the field.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={url('/reenactors-guide/')}>Read the Reenactor's Guide</a> for advice on choosing a
        first musket and matching arms to an impression, or turn to the <a href={url('/ammunition/')}
          >article on ammunition</a
        > for the Minié ball, the paper cartridge and the percussion cap.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="article__aside">
      <section class="box notice">
        <h2 class="box__title">Notice</h2>
        <p style="margin:0">
          <strong>Safety first, last &amp; always.</strong> No projectile of any kind is ever loaded at
          a reenactment. Every event and unit has its own rules for blank cartridges, inspection and
          firing. Know them and obey them.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="box">
        <h2 class="box__title">Contents of This Number</h2>
        <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.1rem">
          {
            categories.map((c) => (
              <li>
                <a href={url(`/categories/${c.slug}/`)}>{c.name}</a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
          <li><a href={url('/ammunition/')}>Ammunition &amp; Ignition</a></li>
          <li><a href={url('/reenactors-guide/')}>The Reenactor's Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href={url('/glossary/')}>Glossary of Terms</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <p class="dingbat" aria-hidden="true">✶ ✶ ✶</p>
  <hr class="rule-double" />

  <div class="front">
    {
      categories.map((cat) => {
        const list = arms.filter((a) => a.data.category === cat.slug);
        return (
          <section class="column">
            <h2>
              <a href={url(`/categories/${cat.slug}/`)}>{cat.name}</a>
            </h2>
            <hr class="rule-short" />
            <p>{cat.blurb}</p>
            {list.map((arm) => (
              <Dispatch arm={arm} />
            ))}
          </section>
        );
      })
    }
    <section class="column">
      <h2><a href={url('/ammunition/')}>Ammunition</a></h2>
      <hr class="rule-short" />
      <p>
        The Minié ball and the paper cartridge, the percussion cap and the new metallic rimfire
        round: how the soldier of 1861–65 loaded and fired, and what the reenactor uses in their
        place.
      </p>
      <article class="dispatch">
        <h3><a href={url('/reenactors-guide/')}>The Reenactor's Guide</a></h3>
        <p>
          Choosing a first musket, matching arms to an impression, and the safety rules that govern
          the field.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
